A||||14||Adrift||Where to get the T-40 Screw used on rear brake calipers?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-14-2001||05:10 PM||||There is one Torx 40 screw used on each of the rear brake calipers. I spent two hours today going to different stores trying to get these screws (mine are stripped). I tried official GM dealer, Canadian Tire, Napa, and a few independent auto part stores.<P><BR>Where can I get two of these screws? Has anyone replaced their before?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ari<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ToddAsh||12-14-2001||05:53 PM|||| Are they the caliper bolts listed on the Fiero store site($14.95)? I just rounded out one of them.Todd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bluemagic||12-14-2001||06:39 PM||||I have some,if you need them send $12 US to cover mailing and handling to Canada.<P>------------------<BR>Keith<BR>85 SE Fiero 4.9L Caddy <BR>345hp||||reg||7
Z||000003||honegod||12-14-2001||07:01 PM||||if you are talking about the screw that holds the parking brake cable bracket to the caliper, it does not need to be special, as in a regular metric bolt the right length will work fine, it is not a high stress job.<BR>I think it is a 9 X 1.25mm ( an 8 X 1.25 felt like it threaded right, but the threads didn't make contact right, too small in diameter)I am not sure on the length, I got a over long one and cut it down to length ( around 1/2 inch long ).||||reg||1
Z||000004||watts||12-15-2001||02:07 PM||||If you're talking about the bolts that actually hold the calipers on - they ARE available from jobbers.  I bought all 4 new ones.  <P>I just called my place, and they say they're sold by Raybestos (part number H5030), and Carlson (might be the same number).<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||12-15-2001||02:39 PM||||you can go to the junkyard and get them for a buck.<P>The bolts should be located in the "help" section that is from motormite.  They carry all those little things in the red packages.<BR>They usually got starter bolts, caliper bolts($12), torque converter bolts, door handles, etc...  at your car parts store like pepboys or autozone||||reg||1
Z||000006||Adrift||12-15-2001||02:47 PM||||I'm not refering to the bolts that hold the caliper on, I'm talking about the bolt (one on each caliper) used to hold the parking brake attachment on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||LS1swap||12-15-2001||07:19 PM||||As honedog said you can replace that with any bolt that will thread in (I did the torx is a pain) I doubt it is 9 mm that would be a pretty uncommon size (10 mm maybe). Its actual description is a low head socket cap screw. For odd fasteners of any kind try <A HREF="http://www.fastenal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fastenal.com</A>  they have just about any fastener in any size and grade. They show 30 + locations in Ontario but no listing for Toronto. Not that familiar with Canada maybe there is one close.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Adrift||12-15-2001||07:38 PM||||This one is in Toronto (basically):<P>111 Martin Ross Ave, Unit # 8 , North York,ON, M3J 2M1<BR>Phone: (416) 665-7085 Fax: (416) 665-7120||||reg||1
Z||000009||David Bartlett||12-16-2001||12:02 PM||||Adrift, I got mine at Canadia Tire in Burlington... the Fairview/Guelph store.  It was in a pack of 4 other torx bits.<P>Give them a call and make sure they still sell them.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Adrift||12-16-2001||12:39 PM||||The screws are available at Canaidan tire, or the drivers to get the screws out? I have gotten the screws out, they're just really old, and I want to replace them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Adrift||12-18-2001||02:03 PM||||fasenal does not carry the screw<BR>any other ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||12-19-2001||12:02 AM||||I had one of those bolts a while back that was a REAL pain. I wrung off two torx bits without budging it, and finally just left the bolt there. The head didn't round out though.||||reg||1
Z||000013||honegod||12-27-2001||09:19 PM||||the screws on all three of the replacement rear calipers that I have recently installed in my 84 ( long ugly story ) used 3/8" x 16 tpi AMERICAN bolts, not metric !!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>( I had a nut on the bolt I used on all 3, I finally took the nut to lowes and found what bolt fit it. )<BR>two 3/4" long grade 8 3/8 X 16 bolts for 50 cents. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||x-thumpr-x||12-27-2001||10:27 PM||||never had a problem with caliper bolts. Little snug at time but with WD-40 and patience, they come off with a snap( or I should say without a snap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)  Now, the bleeder screw is another story! I have to replace my caliper (right rear) becuase the e-brake lever has seized. So I took the same caliper of the parts car, works fine, little lube here and there then go to remover the bleeder SNAP!  So, I just finished drilling out the bleeder (1/2hr job) and put in a repair kit. Praying it works, if not $80 on a new caliper. ||||reg||1
